// Blocked schedule: one worker, outer-loop tiling via `block=`.
//
// Same placement as the naive schedule (single `host` worker, all
// three kernels on it) — the only difference is the `loop y :
// block=4;` directive, which tiles the outer y-axis into chunks of
// 4 rows. The compiler rewrites
//
//     for y in 1..H-1 { ... }
//
// into
//
//     for y__tile in 0..((H-1-1)/4) {
//         for y in 0..4 { ... }
//     }
//
// at the ACFG level (TASK-0030's `apply_block_transforms` pass) and
// the inner-loop iteration tile carries that 4-row chunk size for
// downstream passes.
//
// Why block=4 (a deliberate non-divisible range)
// -----------------------------------------------
// The stencil walks y over `1..H-1`, i.e. 1..15 with H=16 — a range
// of length 14, NOT a multiple of 4. This is chosen on purpose: it
// exercises trailing-remainder tiles. Since TASK-0142, the
// block-transform pass rewrites `block=4` on a length-14 range into
// a static `Sequence[ full-tile nest (3 tiles of 4), trailing
// partial tile (1 tile of 2) ]` — it no longer rejects with
// `BlockTransformError::NotDivisible` (that error is retired). The
// decomposition is iteration-count/order faithful for the
// Petri/boundedness/deadlock consumers, which unroll by static
// range length.
//
// End-to-end status: this `blocked` cell is ACTIVE (not ignored) in
// both the `nucleus/e2e` matrix (a required cell) and the in-crate
// `nucleus/nucleus-compiler/tests/e2e_example_05.rs`. It compiles, builds,
// runs, and is bit-identical to `reference.bin`. Honest scope:
// 05-stencil is single-`host`, so the backend emits from the
// algorithm source (not the block-transformed ACFG) — this cell
// pins the no-reject / no-panic / bit-identical property; the
// tiling *structure* is pinned by the `block_transform` unit and
// integration tests.
//
// Worker placement: still single `host`. PRD §6.3.3: block= is
// orthogonal to worker placement. A multi-worker variant would also
// add a `partition=` directive on the same loop — that's the
// distributed schedule's job, not the blocked one.

schedule for "../prog.algo.nuc" {
    workers = { host };

    place load_image on host;
    place save_image on host;
    place blur3      on host;

    // Tile the outer y-axis into 4-row chunks. The compiler infers
    // the inner-loop range (intra-tile) and rewrites the ACFG into
    // a (tile-loop, intra-tile-loop) nest (TASK-0030).
    loop y : block=4;

    // No transfer directives — single worker, no cross-worker edges.
}
